      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by finding her walking rate in miles per minute. Jana walks one mile in 24 minutes, so her rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Rate} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use this rate to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes. We do this by multiplying her rate by the time:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\left(\\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10 \\text{ minutes}}{24 \\text{ minutes/minute}} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\), we divide both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12}\n\\]\n\nNext, we convert \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) to a decimal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.4\n\\]\n\nThus, Jana will walk approximately 0.4 miles in 10 minutes. \n\nTo present this clearly boxed:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
